t ways to  measure fidelity  and the results can vary significantly  there are three commonly used methods  to measure fidelity that are shown here  on the slide  so on the left there is a colony  screening assay  anger sequencing in the middle and on  the right next generation sequencing  so when comparing fidelity of different  polymerases it is important to note the  method that was used  and only compare values that were  measured using the same methodology and  best if measured under the same  conditions  now collin is creating athletes and  sanger sequencing both require  huge amount of data to estimate  statistically significant and reliable  error rates of high fidelity polymerases  nowadays next generation sequencing  enables analysis of large amounts of  data and simplifies quantification of  statistical data  in this example in the graph  fidelity of dna polymerases was measured  by next generation sequencing  the values were normalized to tags in a  polymerase  so here the value indicates how many  times fidelity of the proofreading dna  polymerase is higher compared to tac  measurements for these polymerases  indicated in the graph were made side by  side under the same conditions allowing  accurate comparison of the results  and let's look at how high fidelity  enzymes can improve your lab  productivity  if you do cloning or sequencing any  sequence accuracy is critical  you want to minimize possibility of  having an error in the pcr sequence  and this is where it is critical to use  a high fidelity polymerase  if you are to choose a pcr enzyme with  the highest fidelity possible  that will increase your confidence  in the dna sequence accuracy after  amplification and save and save your  time from troubleshooting and repetition  of the experiment  displayed on your screen is our first  pull question  what kind of issue have you experienced  while performing pcr  a  incomplete or no amplification  b poor sensitivity and or specificity  errors in pcr am